            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="OXF01" sex="2">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>Jane Elizabeth</forename>
                        <surname>Harley</surname>
                        <addName>Lady Oxford, née Scott</addName>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1774">
                        <placeName> </placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1824"/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Mother of one of <persName key="BUR02">Sir Francis Burdett's</persName> illegitimate children, sister of
    Hobbes Scott and William Scott. Burdett took out a law suit against William Scott in 1811 for
    the recovery of a loan of £5,000 during which Burdett's paternity was revealed. According to J
    Ann Hone, the Oxford children had been known as 'the Harleian Miscellany' on the grounds of their
    doubtful paternity.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="printedbook"> J. Ann Hone,  <title>For the Cause of Truth: Radicalism in London, 1796-1821</title> (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1982). </bibl>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
